// The calendars of silk.time.calendar against reference data.
//
// The rows come from two sources:
// - Unicode ICU 78, through `Intl.DateTimeFormat` of Node 22, for the calendars but Chinese: the
//   fields of days (`i`), and the years (`y`) and the months (`m`) that a walk over every day of
//   1800 to 2200, or 700 to 2900 for the calendars with a rule, gives.
// - The calendar kernel of silkjs, a port of the calendar code of QuickJS, run on its fixture
//   years: years, months, days (`d`), month codes (`c`), eras (`e`), the reference dates of a
//   month and a day (`r`), and the difference of two dates in each unit (`u`).
//
// The standard library differs from the kernel on purpose in four places, and the rows of the
// kernel for them are left out: the Indian calendar has the Gregorian leap rule, so 1900 and 2100
// have no leap day; the Persian calendar has the corrections of ICU from the year 1503; the
// differences of buddhist and roc dates count real days; and the Hebrew `M05L` is no month of a
// year without Adar I.
//
// The Chinese rows are from the kernel only. ICU 78 has about 60 months between 1800 and 2200
// one day off the published calendar, among them the new years of 1954, 2027, and 2030.
//
// Row forms, with fields separated by one space:
//   y calendar year leap daysInYear monthsInYear
//   m calendar year month daysInMonth codeNumber codeLeap
//   d calendar year month day isoYear isoMonth isoDay dayOfYear
//   c calendar year codeNumber codeLeap month      (month -1: the year has no such month)
//   i calendar isoYear isoMonth isoDay year month day era eraYear
//   e calendar era eraYear status year             (status -1: not an era of the calendar)
//   r calendar codeNumber codeLeap day constrain status isoYear isoMonth isoDay
//   u calendar isoYear isoMonth isoDay isoYear isoMonth isoDay unit years months weeks days
//     (unit 4 years, 3 months, 2 weeks, 1 days)
import silk.num.u16

